course: dictionary
lesson: 1716
title: Going round in circles
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Circular means round, like a circle. The word can also describe things that go round in a loop. A route, a walk or an argument can be circular too. The clue is always the loop: something that goes round and comes back to where it began. «We took a circular walk around the lake and ended back at the car park.» The walk did not go on in a straight line, so circular describes a route that loops back.
explain: Look at the words around the word. «She drew a circular table on the board» tells you the shape. Now try a harder one: «His argument was circular: the rule is fair because it is the rule.» The reason is just the claim said again, so the argument goes round in a loop and gets nowhere. Swap your meaning into the sentence: "a loop that gets nowhere" still fits.
explain: Circular can also be a noun. A circular is a letter or advert sent to many people at once, and its plural is circulars. «The school sent a circular about the summer fair.» Ask yourself: is the word describing something, or is it the thing itself?
fact: Circular means shaped like a circle, or round.
fact: A circular route or walk goes round and ends where it started.
fact: A circular argument uses the thing it is trying to prove as its reason.
fact: «Circular breathing» means breathing in through the nose while pushing out air stored in the cheeks, so the sound never stops.
fact: In the UK, «circular signs» give orders, while triangular signs warn and rectangular signs give information.
exercise: «The children ran a circular route around the park.» What does circular describe here? | *A route that loops back to the start | A very fast run | A tired walk home | A straight race to the gate
exercise: «The teacher's reply was circular: the rule is the rule because it says so.» What does circular mean here? | It is shaped like a circle | It is very loud | It is a letter sent to everyone | *It goes round in a loop, with the reason just repeating the claim
exercise: In the UK, which signs give orders? | *Circular signs | Rectangular signs | Triangular signs | Signs with words only
test: «The school sent a circular to every family about the trip.» What is a circular here? | A round table in the hall | *A letter or advert sent to many people at once | A type of bird | A loop that proves nothing
test: Which phrase describes a walk that ends where it started? | A hurried walk | A long walk uphill | A straight walk to the shop | *A circular walk
exam: Which is a circular argument? | An argument with three separate reasons | Two facts that prove a point with evidence | A short list of rules | *It is true because it is true, so the reason is just the claim said again
